          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also known as hypertension, is a common condition where the force of the blood against the walls of the arteries is consistently too high. A normal blood pressure reading is typically around 120/80 mmHg. When blood pressure readings are consistently higher, it can lead to health issues over time, including heart disease and stroke. Most cases of hypertension do not have a single identifiable cause but can be influenced by factors such as age, genetics, body weight, diet, physical inactivity, and stress (source: General clinical reference on hypertension).\n\nA single high reading does not necessarily mean a person has high blood pressure. Blood pressure can fluctuate due to various factors like stress, physical activity, time of day, or even a full bladder. A diagnosis of hypertension usually requires multiple readings taken on different occasions to confirm that the blood pressure is consistently high. This differentiation is crucial because treating a temporary spike isn’t necessary and could lead to unnecessary worry (source: General clinical reference on hypertension). \n\nFor anyone concerned about blood pressure, a discussion with a healthcare provider is recommended for further evaluation and guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
